WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.035
-One of our writers, Karen,
she is 25 years old.

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:06.606
As a result, she has a much
different frame of reference

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:08.741
than I do,
so I thought I'd find out

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:11.010
how much Karen knows
about the past

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.279
in a segment called
"What Does Karen Know?"

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:17.416 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:17.416 --> 00:00:22.188
-Hi, Karen. How are you?
-I'm good. How are you?

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.056
-Good.
I miss seeing you in person,

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:27.327
but it's lovely to see
you virtually this way.

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:30.663
-It's so nice to see you too!
-How has been --

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:34.033
How has the pandemic been
for a 25-year-old?

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:38.304
-Um, I mean for me particularly
as someone who doesn't go out

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:42.408
much to begin with, you know,
just a little bit more sadness.

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:44.277
[ Laughter ]
-Alright.

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:47.246
Are you ready for another trip
down my memory lane

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:50.316
to see if it triggers
a memory in you?

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.417
-Yes, I'm excited.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:53.252
I've done some studying.
-Okay, great.

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:56.356
You've studied the '80s
and the '90s and whatnot?

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:58.424
-Oh.
I think I went back too far.

00:00:58.424 --> 00:00:59.325
Never mind, that's --
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:00.693
How dare you, Karen?

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.061
How dare you?

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:04.397
Alright. Here's our first item.

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:08.000
Does this mean anything to you,
Karen?

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.602
-Um, no.

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:12.004
It almost -- My initial thought
was like a pogo stick,

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:13.639
but that's definitely not true.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.475
-Nah, not a pogo stick.

00:01:15.475 --> 00:01:18.210
-Is it like a water gun
of some kind?

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.813
-It's not a water gun.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:22.649
Karen, it's a Bop It.

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:24.917
Does that mean anything to you?
-Oh, yes, I do.

00:01:24.917 --> 00:01:27.287
I have heard of Bop Its.

00:01:27.287 --> 00:01:29.955
-What have you heard
about the old Bop It?

00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:33.927
-The toy itself tells you
to bop it and then you bop it.

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:35.361
-Yeah.
And it was a hit.

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:36.996
-That's all I know.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:38.664
-What you just described
was popular.

00:01:38.664 --> 00:01:40.800
Of course it's not just Bop It.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.870
You know you also, I think,
yank it, pull it, twist it,

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.639
twist it, flip it, and then
it gets faster and faster.

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:48.641
And right as you think
you finally

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:51.411
outsmarted the Bop It...
-Uh-huh.

00:01:51.411 --> 00:01:52.845
-Game over.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:55.615
Anyway, it didn't have
a very long shelf life,

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.283
but it was a big part
of our lives for probably

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:58.918
like three or four months.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:02.121
Do you know this gentleman?

00:02:02.121 --> 00:02:04.757
-Um, no.
A boxer?

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:08.560
-Mm-hmm. Do you think he's a
real boxer or a fictional boxer?

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.264
-I think he's fictional.
-Gotcha.

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:12.999
-Mm-hmm. And I think --

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.768
-Have you ever seen
any of the "Rocky" films?

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:16.969
-Oh! No!

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:19.672
But I know about Rocky.
[ Laughs ]

00:02:19.672 --> 00:02:22.808
-Well, that's very unimpressive.

00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:26.212
-He gets -- He gets knocked down
and then he gets back up.

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:28.114
-Yeah.
They wrote that song about him.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.016
-"Tubthumping" by --

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:34.287
-Yeah, "Tubthumping"
was the "Rocky" theme song.

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:37.590
He would walk out
and they'd play "Tubthumping."

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.658
By the way,
I can't believe you

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.526
[bleep] pulled "Tubthumping."

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:41.794
[ Laughter ]

00:02:41.794 --> 00:02:43.762
I would have thought 0% chance.

00:02:43.762 --> 00:02:47.667
Do you know who sang
"Tubthumping"?

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:48.901
-Yes. Oh, my God, I do.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:51.270
I know this because it was
an answer at, like,

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.572
a bar trivia thing
I went to a few months ago.

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:55.207
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:57.643
-Chumbawamba!
-Well, there you go.

00:02:57.643 --> 00:03:01.514
Here we are trying to --
That was Ivan Drago.

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.015
Ivan Drago.

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:06.352
And he was a Russian.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:09.355
And what do you think happened
in the fight when they fought?

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:12.558
-Wait, is this not Rocky?
-No, he fought Rocky.

00:03:12.558 --> 00:03:13.592
-Oh, gotcha.

00:03:13.592 --> 00:03:15.361
-Wait, you thought
that was Rocky?

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:18.897
-Yeah, sorry.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.897 --> 00:03:21.267
-Again, I just can't --
Your brain both

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:27.707
has room for Chumbawamba
and not Sylvester Stallone.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:31.010
-Oh, Sylvester Stallone, okay.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.011
-Never -- We're going to move on
to the next one.

00:03:33.011 --> 00:03:36.248
No chance.
Do you know who this is?

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:37.450
-Oh, no.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.652
-It looks like Ivan Drago
going to a party.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.688
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.289
Probably shouldn't have put
those two next to each other.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.826
Does Max Headroom
mean anything to you?

00:03:46.826 --> 00:03:49.262
-No, yeah, sorry.
-That's Max Headroom.

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:54.433
He was basically presented to us
as sort of an A.I.-type fella.

00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:58.304
He had a show, a very brief show
but then more importantly became

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:02.709
a sort of television pitchman
for New Coke.

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.477
-Like, the drink?
-Yeah.

00:04:04.477 --> 00:04:07.179
-Oh. Fun.
-Did you hear about New Coke?

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:08.414
Did you hear about this?

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:13.019
-Is it -- By now is it just
what we call Coke?

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:15.187
-There was New Coke.
-Okay.

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.456
-Then they went back
to Coke Classic, right?

00:04:17.456 --> 00:04:18.724
-Yes, that's where we are now.

00:04:18.724 --> 00:04:20.192
-We had Coke Classic.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:22.562
-But we just call it Coke.
-Right.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:23.730
-Alright.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.465
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:29.935
Do you know who this is?

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:32.404
-Oh, no. I'm sorry.

00:04:32.404 --> 00:04:33.806
But she looks so nice.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.475
-You think she looks nice? Okay.

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:37.476
[ Laughter ]

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:42.448
Based on her look, what would
you guess she did for a living?

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.351
-Probably owned lots
of property?

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:46.652
-Okay, I think technically

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:49.689
there was some property issues
with what she was doing.

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:51.057
-Okay.

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:53.025
Was she like some sort of host,
presenter-type person?

00:04:53.025 --> 00:04:54.727
-Yeah, she had
a television show.

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:57.196
Her and her husband
were presenters.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.430
-Okay.
-And what do you think

00:04:58.430 --> 00:04:59.532
they were talking about?

00:04:59.532 --> 00:05:00.699
When you see a lady like that,

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:03.902
what do you think
she's telling you to do?

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:07.073
-To get your life in order.
-Yes.

00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:08.941
There was some of that.

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:12.044
And if you were to follow
one person

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:14.847
to get your life in order...
-Oh, Jesus!

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:17.216
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:18.584
-My grandma will be so happy.

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:23.990
-Yeah, that was Tammy Faye
Bakker, and she was married

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:29.495
to a hot piece of garbage who's
closer to the devil than Jesus.

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:30.729
I think that's safe
to say, right?

00:05:30.729 --> 00:05:32.064
-Sure.
-Yeah.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:35.934
And, yeah, they were --
they had a television show.

00:05:35.934 --> 00:05:37.670
Tammy Faye and Jim Bakker.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.373
And Jim's still around,
and she is sadly --

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:41.674
rest in peace,
Tammy Faye Bakker.

00:05:41.674 --> 00:05:44.143
I think she got
the raw end of the deal.

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:45.844
That's where I land on that one.

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:47.280
Here's our next one.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.147
Who's this?

00:05:49.147 --> 00:05:51.017
-Ooh, just a cool guy.

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:52.585
-He is a cool guy.

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:55.755
Cool is actually
half of his name.

00:05:55.755 --> 00:05:56.855
-Is it Mr. Cool?

00:05:56.855 --> 00:05:58.291
-No.
-Oh.

00:05:58.291 --> 00:06:02.328
-But you're never going to get
it, so don't just keep guessing.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.597
-Cool Red.
I'm going to keep guessing.

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:06.732
-Please, Karen, I'm telling you
you're not going to get it.

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:08.267
[ Laughter ]
-Cool Dude.

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:11.303
-Please, Karen, this is going
to go on forever.

00:06:11.303 --> 00:06:13.573
-Cool Bug.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:16.776
-He was a pitchman as well,
also for soda.

00:06:16.776 --> 00:06:21.046
What soda was this gentleman --
His name is Cool Spot.

00:06:21.046 --> 00:06:22.848
-Mountain Dew.
-No.

00:06:22.848 --> 00:06:25.451
But you're thinking
of the right color can,

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:27.052
but it's not Mountain Dew.

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:29.388
-Sprite?
-Again, same color,

00:06:29.388 --> 00:06:33.826
but amazingly you're going
to guess the right one last.

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:36.695
-Wait, what? There's
Mountain Dew, there's Sprite.

00:06:36.695 --> 00:06:38.630
There's another one
that's green and yellow?

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:42.268
Or green and red, sorry.
-1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6...

00:06:42.268 --> 00:06:43.569
[ Laughter ]

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:45.204
-8, 8.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:46.439
Wait, what did you just say?

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:48.107
-I said, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6...

00:06:48.107 --> 00:06:49.542
-7-Up!
-There we go.

00:06:49.542 --> 00:06:50.576
[ Laughter ]

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:52.711
He was the 7-Up Cool Spot.

00:06:52.711 --> 00:06:55.314
And that was probably
my early teens,

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:56.616
and that's the kind of gentleman

00:06:56.616 --> 00:07:00.086
I needed
to tell me what to be drinking.

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:01.554
-Nice.
-Again, it was him or that --

00:07:01.554 --> 00:07:03.088
or Max Headroom.

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:04.857
I didn't have a lot of choices.
-Right. Cool Dude.

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:09.661
-Well, once again I feel like
we've accomplished not a ton,

00:07:09.661 --> 00:07:12.031
but I think we've enjoyed
our time together.

00:07:12.031 --> 00:07:14.733
-Yeah, this has been really fun.
Oh, but there's more.

00:07:14.733 --> 00:07:15.868
-Oh, right.
-Yes.

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.770
-This is the part
I always forget about.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.306
Now you, a person of
a different generation,

00:07:20.306 --> 00:07:22.875
is going to see
if I know anything about your --

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:25.010
the things in your life.
-Yes.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:26.579
These are things that were
important to me growing up.

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:27.780
-Okay, let's take a look.
-And just to be clear,

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:29.916
you've never seen
these pictures before.

00:07:29.916 --> 00:07:31.116
-Alright.

00:07:31.116 --> 00:07:33.285
-Do you know who she is?

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:36.155
-That looks like
a young Tammy Faye Bakker.

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:37.356
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.356 --> 00:07:39.791
On her way to school.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:07:39.791 --> 00:07:41.026
-That's a children's book.

00:07:41.026 --> 00:07:42.928
Is it a children's
book character?

00:07:42.928 --> 00:07:44.830
-Mm-hmm.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.666
-God, we're reading some kids
books now because of the boys,

00:07:47.666 --> 00:07:50.168
but I don't think
we've read this one.

00:07:50.168 --> 00:07:52.838
Is she a crime solver?

00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:55.507
-Oh, actually kind of
in one of the books.

00:07:55.507 --> 00:07:57.476
So, this is Kit Kittredge.

00:07:57.476 --> 00:07:59.812
-Kit Kittredge. Okay.
-Mm-hmm.

00:07:59.812 --> 00:08:01.680
She's an American Girl doll.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.649
-Oh, she's
an American Girl doll.

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:05.050
-Yeah, yeah.
Well, this is the book

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:06.452
before the doll,
but she's great.

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:08.521
She lived during
the Great Depression,

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:10.255
and she was really into
writing and stuff.

00:08:10.255 --> 00:08:13.625
-I'm glad she can be so upbeat
while the country starved.

00:08:13.625 --> 00:08:15.127
[ Laughter ]
-It's something --

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:17.496
-Glad things are
going okay for Kit.

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:21.066
-[ Laughs ]

00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:23.969
-Probably not a crossover
with "The Grapes of Wrath."

00:08:23.969 --> 00:08:25.738
-[ Laughs ] I feel like --
-Alright.

00:08:25.738 --> 00:08:28.473
Let's take a look at one more.

00:08:28.473 --> 00:08:32.844
Oh, those are -- these are,
like, talking vegetables, right?

00:08:32.844 --> 00:08:34.113
-Yeah! It's "VeggieTales."

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:36.315
-Is it some sort of pun off
vegetables or something?

00:08:36.315 --> 00:08:37.549
-Yeah.

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:40.285
-The Veget-- the Vegetepeoples?

00:08:40.285 --> 00:08:42.221
-No, but keep going.

00:08:42.221 --> 00:08:46.292
-The Vegete-- the Vegete--
the Vegetebros?

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:47.927
[ Laughter ]

00:08:51.096 --> 00:08:53.099
Vegete-- I don't know.

00:08:53.099 --> 00:08:55.300
-It's called "VeggieTales."

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:56.835
-"VeggieTales."

00:08:56.835 --> 00:08:58.170
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.140
-It's great because they
tell you a lot of Bible stories.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:02.841
-Do they really?
-Mm-hmm.

00:09:02.841 --> 00:09:06.111
-So they're the real
Tammy Faye Bakker in all this.

00:09:06.111 --> 00:09:08.414
-Yeah, they're very effective.

00:09:08.414 --> 00:09:09.982
-They're effective?
-Yeah.

00:09:09.982 --> 00:09:13.552
-Well, I guess in the end
that was probably the reason

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:15.754
we didn't go to church
that much in our family is

00:09:15.754 --> 00:09:17.656
no vegetables
told us the upside.

00:09:17.656 --> 00:09:19.124
[ Laughter ]

00:09:19.124 --> 00:09:20.325
Karen, once again, I feel like

00:09:20.325 --> 00:09:22.094
we're even closer
than we were before

00:09:22.094 --> 00:09:23.729
because we've shared

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:26.932
our different
generational attractions.

00:09:26.932 --> 00:09:30.102
Thank you so much for the time.
-Bye! Thank you!

00:09:30.102 --> 00:09:31.837
-This has been
"What Does Karen Know?"

00:09:31.837 --> 00:09:34.206 line:0%
♪♪

